Questions for Pat Hayes — IKL semantics as formalized in Factoidal's Lean 4 tree
Status: DRAFT, held — not sent anywhere. Owner will decide if/when/how to use.
Date: 2026-08-25. Context: https://github.com/danbri/factoidal/issues/598
(machine-checked unified LBase/IKL model theory), issues 580/589 (CL/IKL
component, proposition individuation). Each question states the source text,
what we currently do, and what an answer would settle. References: IKL GUIDE
(https://www.ihmc.us/users/phayes/IKL/GUIDE/GUIDE.html), IKL Specification
[IKLSPEC], LBase (https://www.w3.org/TR/lbase/), ISO/IEC 24707.

Q1. Proposition individuation: naming between the semantic floor and =p.
  Source: GUIDE Appendix B fixes the semantic minimum (bound-variable
  renaming does not change the proposition; the proposition depends on the
  things named, not the names) and deliberately leaves anything stronger to
  the defined relation =p.
  What we do: proposition named graphs are keyed by SHA-256 of the
  alpha-normalized canonical CLIF sentence — so the floor's condition (1)
  holds by construction; condition (2) (co-referring names) is not
  syntactically decidable and is left to equality reasoning; =p variants
  (commuted conjunctions etc.) get distinct graph names today.
  Question: for a system that must give propositions stable public names
  (IRIs), is keying on the alpha-normalized sentence the intended reading of
  the floor, or did you envisage implementations keying on the =p normal
  form — accepting that the name then identifies more than the semantic
  minimum requires? Is there a reason to prefer one as the interchange
  convention?

Q2. The exact =p axiom set and its closure.
  Source: GUIDE, "Identity between propositions": =p is reflexive,
  symmetric, transitive, "based on work by John Sowa", with listed
  identities (and-commutativity, the or/not-and identity,
  associativity flattening), and the intent that =p be checkable "by a
  reasonably fast computation on the inner sentences".
  What we plan: implement =p as an executable normalizer (sort conjuncts,
  flatten associativity, the listed de Morgan identity, alpha-normalize).
  Question: is the GUIDE's identity list intended to be exhaustive for =p,
  or exemplary of a longer Sowa-derived set? If a canonical list exists
  (published or not), which identities are in it? Was double-negation
  elimination in scope?

Q3. The phrase layer in IKL practice.
  Source: ISO/IEC 24707 gives texts phrase structure (cl:text, cl:module,
  cl:imports, cl:comment). The GUIDE's examples are bare sentence
  sequences.
  What we do: our reader parses bare sentence sequences and rejects the
  phrase forms with an explicit error (tracked, deliberate boundary).
  Question: did IKL practice/intended use assume the CL phrase layer
  (modules, imports) or treat texts as flat sentence sequences? Is
  rejecting the phrase forms a defensible interchange boundary, or does
  real IKL content in the wild depend on them?

Q4. Statements about statements: named graphs and RDF 1.2 triple terms as
  the operational rendering of that-terms.
  What we do: every sentence S maps to a named graph (content plus the
  canonical sentence recorded as a literal inside it), decorated from the
  default graph — cl:asserts for assertion, plain link triples for
  predications like believes/ist (which do NOT assert their content), and,
  where S is a single atom with translation triple t, an RDF 1.2 bridge
  <g> rdf:reifies <<t>>. Querying "what holds" is done by an entailment
  regime that lifts only asserts-decorated graph content.
  Question: does this decorated-graph rendering match your intent for how
  ist/contexts and proposition names should land in an RDF-family system?
  Anything in it you would flag as a semantic mistake rather than a
  representation choice?

Q5. A machine-checked model construction as a review artifact.
  What we plan: constructing, inside Lean 4, an interpretation satisfying
  the IKL structural axioms (GUIDE Appendix A) — a mechanized consistency
  witness for the semantics; deviations discovered en route would be
  findings about the documents' intent.
  Question: would you be willing to review such a construction against
  intent — in particular the treatment of the that-operator's
  interpretation (our iProp is keyed by the canonical sentence, with
  alpha-invariance imposed as an interpretation condition), and the
  handling of paradoxical "definitions" as unsatisfiable rather than
  detected?
